 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A physical quantity is something we can measure, with a number and a unit. The SI system has seven base quantities; in Form 2 we use five:\n• length: metre (m), measured with a ruler, metre rule or tape measure;\n• mass: kilogram (kg), measured with a balance;\n• time: second (s), measured with a stopwatch or clock;\n• temperature: kelvin (K), measured with a thermometer (we also use °C);\n• electric current: ampere (A), measured with an ammeter.\n(The other two are the amount of substance, mole, and luminous intensity, candela.)\nOther quantities are derived from these: area (m²), volume (m³), speed (m/s), density (kg/m³).\nPrefixes: kilo- = 1000 (1 km = 1000 m), centi- = one hundredth (100 cm = 1 m), milli- = one thousandth (1000 mm = 1 m).",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {
   "a": 2.5,
   "b": 350
  },
  "problem": "Write {a} km in metres and {b} cm in metres.",
  "steps": [
   "Kilo means a thousand: {a} km = {a} × {factor('km', 'm')} = {convertUnit(a, 'km', 'm')} m.",
   "Centi means one hundredth: {b} cm = {b} ÷ {factor('m', 'cm')} = {convertUnit(b, 'cm', 'm')} m."
  ],
  "answer": "{convertUnit(a, 'km', 'm')} m and {convertUnit(b, 'cm', 'm')} m",
  "checks": []
 },
